DIAGNOSTIC: re-enable the rear camera to bisect the fault

WHY
---
The front one is in a dead end: the sensor emits, CSIPHY1 hears it on all five
lanes, the whole CSIPHY is by now identical to the vendor's -lane table 115 out
of 115, common block, clocks, reset- and not a single start-of-transmission sync
reaches the CSID.

The question that separates the two possibilities is still missing: is it OUR
camera that fails, or is it CAMSS that fails on this SoC? And to answer it we
need a second sensor on a different CSIPHY.

We have one: the rear camera (IMX682) has had a driver since 0050 and goes to
CSIPHY0. It was taken out of the device tree in 0051 because it was believed to
be C-PHY, and that dismissal rests on reasoning that is NOT solid: "three lanes,
therefore C-PHY". Three-lane D-PHY exists and is legal.

What the two cameras' factory files actually say:

    rear     laneAssign = 0x210    three nibbles, identity mapping
    front    laneAssign = 0x3210   four nibbles

and the register that REALLY tells D-PHY from C-PHY, 0x0111, DOES NOT APPEAR in
either one's tables: both use the sensor's default value. On the front one that
value, read live over I2C, is 0x02, that is, D-PHY.

Besides, the rear camera's "no frames arrive" was measured in August BEFORE all
the CSIPHY fixes from this investigation (0060, 0063, 0065, 0066). It has never
been tried again since.

WHAT IT DOES
------------
It puts the rear camera back into the device tree, in parallel with the front
one, each on its own CSIPHY. Both at once, which is what the CAMSS async
notifier requires.

HOW TO READ THE RESULT
----------------------
  the rear one gives SOT on csiphy0  -> CAMSS works on this SoC and the problem
                                        is in the front camera's path
  it gives nothing either            -> the problem is CAMSS's or the SoC's, and
                                        there is nothing special about the front
                                        camera

This does NOT fix anything: it is to separate two hypotheses that have been
stuck together for the whole investigation.
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150-xiaomi-surya.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150-xiaomi-surya.dtsi
@@ -227,5 +227,12 @@
 		 * /dev/media0. O los dos, o ninguno.
 		 */
+		port@0 {
+			csiphy0_ep: endpoint {
+				data-lanes = <0 1>;
+				remote-endpoint = <&imx682_ep>;
+			};
+		};
+
 		port@1 {
 			csiphy1_ep: endpoint {
 				clock-lanes = <7>;
@@ -251,6 +258,36 @@
 	 *
 	 * Sony IMX682 Main @ 1a  (C-PHY, inutilizable con CAMSS)
 	 */
+
+	/*
+	 * REACTIVADA para bisecar: hace falta un segundo sensor en otro CSIPHY
+	 * para separar "falla nuestra camara" de "falla CAMSS en este SoC".
+	 */
+	camera@1a {
+		compatible = "sony,imx682";
+		reg = <0x1a>;
+
+		reset-gpios = <&tlmm 25 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		pinctrl-0 = <&cam_mclk1_active>;
+
+		clocks = <&camcc CAMCC_MCLK1_CLK>;
+		clock-names = "xvclk";
+		assigned-clocks = <&camcc CAMCC_MCLK1_CLK>;
+		assigned-clock-rates = <24000000>;
+
+		dovdd-supply = <&vreg_l13a_1p8>;
+		avdd-supply = <&vreg_wide_ldo_avdd2>;
+		dvdd-supply = <&wl_dvdd1>;
+
+		port {
+			imx682_ep: endpoint {
+				data-lanes = <1 2>;
+				link-frequencies = /bits/ 64 <1020000000>;
+				remote-endpoint = <&csiphy0_ep>;
+			};
+		};
+	};
